      "content": "1. Himalayan Mountain Ranges"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Himalayas are young fold mountains structurally divided into Greater Himalayas (Himadri), Lesser Himalayas (Himachal), and Outer Himalayas (Shiwaliks).",
        "Anamudi (2,695 m) in the Anaimalai Hills is the highest peak in Peninsular India.",
        "The Peninsular Plateau is broadly divided into the Central Highlands lying north of the Narmada River and the Deccan Plateau lying south of it.",
        "Strategic Himalayan passes such as Zoji La, Shipki La, Nathu La, and Jelep La connect India with Tibet and Central Asia.",
        "Intermontane plateaus are enclosed by mountains (e.g., Ladakh Plateau), while Piedmont plateaus sit at the foot of mountains (e.g., Malwa Plateau)."
      ],
      "id": "le3y4xm"
    },
